window.LB = window.LB || {};
LB["p5-water"] = {
  notes: [
    { h: "Three states of water", t: "Water can exist in three states: solid (ice), liquid (water) and gas (water vapour). Ice has a definite shape and a definite volume. Liquid water has a definite volume but no definite shape. Water vapour has no definite shape and no definite volume.", kw: ["solid", "liquid", "gas", "water vapour"] },
    { h: "Water vapour is invisible", t: "Water vapour is a gas and it cannot be seen. The white ‘steam’ you see a short distance above a boiling kettle is NOT water vapour. It is made of tiny water droplets formed when hot water vapour loses heat to the cooler surrounding air and condenses. The clear gap just above the spout is invisible water vapour.", kw: ["invisible", "tiny water droplets", "condenses"] },
    { h: "Melting", t: "Melting is the change from solid to liquid. Ice gains heat and melts into water at 0 °C, its melting point. While the ice is melting, its temperature stays at 0 °C even though it is still gaining heat, because the heat gained is used to change the ice into water.", kw: ["gains heat", "melts", "melting point"] },
    { h: "Freezing", t: "Freezing is the change from liquid to solid. Water loses heat and freezes into ice at 0 °C, its freezing point. While the water is freezing, its temperature stays at 0 °C even though it is still losing heat. Water in an ice tray in the freezer loses heat to the colder surroundings.", kw: ["loses heat", "freezes", "freezing point"] },
    { h: "Boiling", t: "Boiling is the change from liquid to gas that takes place throughout the liquid at a fixed temperature, the boiling point. Pure water boils at 100 °C. Bubbles of water vapour form inside the water. While water is boiling, its temperature stays at 100 °C even though it is still gaining heat; heating it more strongly only makes it boil away faster.", kw: ["gains heat", "boils", "boiling point", "bubbles"] },
    { h: "Evaporation", t: "Evaporation is the change from liquid water to water vapour that takes place at any temperature and only at the exposed surface of the water. The water gains heat from the surroundings as it evaporates. Wet clothes drying, puddles drying up and a wet floor drying after mopping are examples of evaporation.", kw: ["any temperature", "exposed surface", "gains heat", "water vapour"] },
    { h: "Evaporation vs boiling", t: "Both evaporation and boiling are changes from liquid to gas in which water gains heat. Boiling takes place only at the boiling point (100 °C for water) and throughout the liquid, with bubbles. Evaporation takes place at any temperature, only at the surface, and is usually slow with no bubbles.", kw: ["any temperature", "surface", "throughout the liquid"] },
    { h: "Condensation", t: "Condensation is the change from gas to liquid. When water vapour comes into contact with a cooler surface, it loses heat to the cooler surface and condenses into water droplets. Examples: droplets on the underside of a pot lid, misting of a bathroom mirror after a hot shower, and dew on grass in the early morning.", kw: ["loses heat", "cooler surface", "condenses", "water droplets"] },
    { h: "Droplets on a cold glass", t: "Water droplets form on the OUTER surface of a glass of iced water. They come from the water vapour in the surrounding air, which loses heat to the cooler outer surface of the glass and condenses into water droplets. They do not come from the water inside, which cannot pass through the glass, so the mass of the glass and its contents increases.", kw: ["water vapour in the surrounding air", "cooler outer surface", "condenses"] },
    { h: "Misting of spectacles", t: "When a person steps out of an air-conditioned room into warm, humid air, the lenses of the spectacles are cooler than the surrounding air. Warm water vapour in the surrounding air loses heat to the cooler lenses and condenses into tiny water droplets, so the lenses mist up. The same happens when the person bends over a bowl of hot soup: water vapour from the soup condenses on the cooler lenses.", kw: ["cooler lenses", "loses heat", "condenses", "water droplets"] },
    { h: "Factors affecting the rate of evaporation", t: "Water evaporates faster when (1) the temperature of the surroundings is higher, (2) the exposed surface area of the water is larger, (3) there is wind or moving air, and (4) the surrounding air is less humid (has less water vapour). In a fair test, change only ONE of these factors and keep the rest, including the amount of water, the same.", kw: ["higher temperature", "larger exposed surface area", "moving air", "less humid"] },
    { h: "Uses of evaporation", t: "We dry clothes by spreading them out in a sunny, windy place so that water evaporates faster. When sweat evaporates, it gains heat from our body, so the body loses heat and we feel cooler. A fan makes us feel cooler because the moving air increases the rate of evaporation of sweat, not because it blows out cold air.", kw: ["gains heat from our body", "feel cooler", "rate of evaporation"] }
  ],
  traps: [
    "‘Steam’ above a kettle is water vapour → the white mist is tiny water droplets; water vapour itself is invisible.",
    "Droplets on a cold glass seep through the glass → they come from water vapour in the SURROUNDING AIR that condenses on the cooler outer surface.",
    "Condensation happens because water vapour gains ‘coldness’ → water vapour LOSES HEAT to a cooler surface and condenses.",
    "Water keeps getting hotter while it boils → its temperature stays at 100 °C; the heat gained is used to change water into water vapour.",
    "Flat part of a heating graph means no heat is gained → heat is still gained but is used for the change of state.",
    "Evaporation needs the Sun or 100 °C → evaporation takes place at ANY temperature, only at the exposed surface.",
    "More water in the dish means it dries faster → a LARGER EXPOSED SURFACE AREA (not more water) increases the rate of evaporation.",
    "A fan cools us by blowing cold air → the moving air increases the rate of evaporation of sweat, which gains heat from our body."
  ],
  lessons: [
    {
      h: "Reading heating and cooling curves",
      concept: "On a temperature–time graph, a sloping part means the temperature is changing and the water is in one state. A flat part means a change of state: at 0 °C (melting or freezing) or at 100 °C (boiling). During a flat part, heat is still being gained (or lost), but it is used to change the state, so the temperature stays the same.",
      example: {
        q: "A beaker of water was heated over a steady flame. The graph shows its temperature. (a) At what temperature did the water boil? (b) Explain why the temperature stayed the same from 9 min to 15 min although the flame was still lit.",
        fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 25], ["3", 50], ["6", 75], ["9", 100], ["12", 100], ["15", 100]] },
        marks: 3,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Find the flat part of the line. It is at 100 °C, from 9 min onwards.",
          "Step 2: A flat part at 100 °C means the water is boiling, a change of state from liquid to gas.",
          "Step 3: The flame is still lit, so the water is still GAINING heat. Say so clearly.",
          "Step 4: Say what the heat is used for: changing liquid water into water vapour."
        ],
        answer: "(a) 100 °C. (b) The water was still gaining heat from the flame, but the heat gained was used to change the water into water vapour, so its temperature stayed at 100 °C."
      },
      tip: "Never write ‘no heat was gained’ for a flat part. Write ‘still gaining heat, but the heat is used to change … into …’.",
      try: {
        q: "Warm water was heated over a steady flame. The graph shows its temperature. Which statement about the water from 4 min to 8 min is correct?",
        fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 40], ["2", 70], ["4", 100], ["6", 100], ["8", 100]] },
        o: ["It stopped gaining heat.", "It gained heat and changed into water vapour.", "It lost heat and condensed.", "It gained heat and its temperature rose."],
        a: 1,
        why: "The flat part at 100 °C shows boiling. The flame is still lit, so the water keeps gaining heat, but the heat is used to change water into water vapour, so the temperature does not rise."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Explaining condensation fully",
      concept: "A full-mark condensation answer has a chain of four links: (1) WHERE the water vapour comes from (the surrounding air, hot soup, a shower), (2) it comes into contact with a COOLER surface, (3) it LOSES HEAT to that surface, (4) it CONDENSES into water droplets. Missing the source or the heat loss is the most common reason for losing a mark.",
      example: {
        q: "Aisha took a can of cold drink out of the fridge and left it on a table. After a few minutes, water droplets appeared on the outer surface of the can. Explain how the water droplets were formed.",
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Source: the water did not come from inside the can. It came from water vapour in the surrounding air.",
          "Step 2: The outer surface of the can is cooler than the surrounding air.",
          "Step 3: The water vapour loses heat to the cooler surface and condenses into water droplets."
        ],
        answer: "Water vapour in the surrounding air came into contact with the cooler outer surface of the can. It lost heat to the cooler surface and condensed into water droplets."
      },
      tip: "Learn the sentence: ‘water vapour in the surrounding air comes into contact with the cooler surface, loses heat and condenses into water droplets’.",
      try: {
        q: "Mrs Goh’s spectacles misted up when she walked out of an air-conditioned cinema into the hot afternoon. Which explanation would score full marks?",
        o: ["The cold lenses gave out water vapour, which then turned into water droplets on them.", "Water vapour in the surrounding air lost heat to the cooler lenses and condensed.", "The warm air made the lenses gain heat, so the water inside them evaporated out.", "Water vapour in the air gained heat from the cool lenses and condensed on them."],
        a: 1,
        why: "It names the source (surrounding air), the cooler lenses, the heat loss and the condensation. Water vapour loses heat, not gains heat, when it condenses."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Evaporation vs boiling",
      concept: "Both are changes from liquid water to water vapour, and in both the water gains heat. The differences are what examiners test: evaporation takes place at ANY temperature and ONLY at the exposed surface; boiling takes place only at the boiling point (100 °C for water) and THROUGHOUT the liquid, with bubbles forming. Evaporation is usually slow; boiling is fast.",
      example: {
        q: "State two differences between evaporation and boiling.",
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: Compare the temperature: evaporation at any temperature; boiling at a fixed temperature, 100 °C.",
          "Step 2: Compare where it happens: evaporation only at the surface; boiling throughout the liquid.",
          "Step 3: Write each difference as a pair so the marker sees both sides."
        ],
        answer: "Evaporation can take place at any temperature, but boiling takes place only at the boiling point of 100 °C. Evaporation takes place only at the exposed surface of the water, but boiling takes place throughout the water."
      },
      tip: "Always write a difference as ‘X … but Y …’. Saying only one side (e.g. ‘boiling is at 100 °C’) may not earn the mark.",
      try: {
        q: "Which of these is an example of boiling rather than evaporation?",
        o: ["A puddle drying up on the road on a hot afternoon", "Bubbles forming throughout the water in a kettle at 100 °C", "Wet hair drying quickly under a fan in the bedroom", "Sweat drying on the skin after a run in the sun"],
        a: 1,
        why: "Boiling takes place throughout the liquid at 100 °C, with bubbles. The other three happen below 100 °C and only at the surface, so they are evaporation."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Fair tests on the rate of evaporation",
      concept: "To test one factor (temperature, exposed surface area, moving air or humidity), change ONLY that factor. Keep the others the same, including the amount of water at the start. Measure the rate by the mass of water lost in a fixed time, or the time taken for the water to dry up. A larger mass lost (or a shorter time) means a faster rate of evaporation.",
      example: {
        q: "Ravi set up four dishes as shown, each with 50 ml of water. He wants to find out if the exposed surface area of water affects the rate of evaporation. (a) Which two set-ups should he use? (b) Explain why.",
        fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "50 ml water", "Under a fan"] }, { label: "B", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "50 ml water", "Under a fan"] }, { label: "C", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "50 ml water", "No fan"] }, { label: "D", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "50 ml water", "In the sun"] }] },
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: The variable to change is the exposed surface area, so the containers must differ (wide dish vs narrow beaker).",
          "Step 2: Look for a pair where everything else is the same. A and B are both under a fan with 50 ml of water.",
          "Step 3: Check other pairs: C and D differ in container AND place (no fan vs in the sun), so they are not fair."
        ],
        answer: "(a) A and B. (b) Only the exposed surface area of the water is different; the amount of water and the place (under a fan) are the same, so it is a fair test."
      },
      tip: "Before choosing a pair, cover the variable being tested and check that EVERY other line is identical.",
      try: {
        q: "Four identical towels were soaked in the same amount of water and hung up. Which pair shows the effect of moving air on the rate of evaporation?",
        tbl: [["Towel", "Place", "Fan"], ["P", "Sunny corridor", "On"], ["Q", "Shady room", "On"], ["R", "Sunny corridor", "Off"], ["S", "Shady room", "Off"]],
        o: ["P and Q", "P and S", "Q and R", "P and R"],
        a: 3,
        why: "P and R are both in the sunny corridor; only the fan (moving air) is different. Q and S would also work, but P and S and Q and R change two things."
      }
    },
    {
      h: "Evaporation causes cooling",
      concept: "When water evaporates, it GAINS HEAT from the surface it is on. So the surface LOSES heat and becomes cooler. That is why sweat cools the body, why a wet cloth around a thermometer bulb gives a lower reading, and why a fan (which increases the rate of evaporation) makes a wet surface even cooler.",
      example: {
        q: "After a run, Jun Wei feels cooler when he sits under a fan. Explain why.",
        marks: 2,
        think: [
          "Step 1: His skin is covered with sweat.",
          "Step 2: The moving air from the fan increases the rate of evaporation of the sweat.",
          "Step 3: As the sweat evaporates, it gains heat from his body, so his body loses heat and he feels cooler."
        ],
        answer: "The moving air from the fan increases the rate of evaporation of the sweat on his skin. As the sweat evaporates, it gains heat from his body, so his body loses heat and he feels cooler."
      },
      tip: "Name the direction of heat: sweat GAINS heat FROM the body. ‘The fan blows cold air’ scores zero.",
      try: {
        q: "Thermometer bulb P was left dry. Bulb Q was wrapped in a wet cloth. Both were placed in the same room. Why did Q show a lower reading after 10 minutes?",
        o: ["Water in the cloth condensed and lost heat to the bulb.", "The cloth trapped cold air around the bulb.", "Water in the cloth evaporated and gained heat from the bulb.", "The wet cloth stopped the bulb from gaining any heat."],
        a: 2,
        why: "As water evaporates from the cloth, it gains heat from the bulb, so the bulb loses heat and its reading drops. Condensation would give out heat, not remove it."
      }
    }
  ],
  mcq: [
    { q: "In which state is water vapour?", o: ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas", "It is not a state of matter"], a: 2, why: "Water vapour is water in the gaseous state. It is invisible and has no definite shape or volume.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "At what temperature does pure water boil?", o: ["0 °C", "37 °C", "50 °C", "100 °C"], a: 3, why: "The boiling point of pure water is 100 °C. 0 °C is its melting/freezing point.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What is the change from ice to water called?", o: ["Melting", "Freezing", "Evaporation", "Condensation"], a: 0, why: "Solid to liquid is melting. Freezing is the opposite, liquid to solid.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "At what temperature does water freeze?", o: ["100 °C", "37 °C", "10 °C", "0 °C"], a: 3, why: "Water freezes at 0 °C, which is the same temperature at which ice melts.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "What happens to water when it evaporates?", o: ["It loses heat and changes into water vapour.", "It gains heat and changes into water droplets.", "It loses heat and changes into ice.", "It gains heat and changes into water vapour."], a: 3, why: "During evaporation, liquid water gains heat and changes into water vapour, a gas.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "The canteen floor was wet after it was mopped. An hour later, it was dry. Which process made the floor dry?", o: ["Condensation", "Melting", "Freezing", "Evaporation"], a: 3, why: "The water on the floor gained heat from the surroundings and changed into water vapour at its surface: evaporation.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "The graph shows the temperature of some water as it was heated. At what temperature did the water boil?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 28], ["2", 52], ["4", 76], ["6", 100], ["8", 100], ["10", 100]] }, o: ["28 °C", "76 °C", "100 °C", "It did not boil"], a: 2, why: "The temperature stops rising and stays flat at 100 °C from 6 min, which shows the water is boiling.", lvl: 1 },
    { q: "Four dishes each held 50 g of water and were left in the sun for a day. The graph shows the mass of water LEFT in each dish. Which dish lost the most water?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Dish", yl: "Mass of water left (g)", bars: [["P", 38], ["Q", 20], ["R", 44], ["S", 12]] }, o: ["P", "Q", "R", "S"], a: 3, why: "All started with 50 g. S has the least left (12 g), so it lost the most (38 g). Do not pick the tallest bar — that is the dish that lost the least.", lvl: 1 },

    { q: "A kettle of water is boiling. What is the white ‘steam’ seen a short distance above the spout made of?", o: ["Water vapour", "Smoke", "Tiny water droplets", "Hot air"], a: 2, why: "Water vapour is invisible. The white mist is tiny water droplets formed when the hot water vapour loses heat to the cooler air and condenses.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which statement is true of evaporation but NOT of boiling?", o: ["Water gains heat.", "Water changes into water vapour.", "There is a change of state.", "It can take place at any temperature."], a: 3, why: "Both involve gaining heat and changing into water vapour. Only evaporation happens at any temperature; boiling needs 100 °C.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Four containers of different shapes each held 100 g of water and were placed side by side on a window ledge. The graph shows the mass of water left after 6 hours. Which container most likely had the largest exposed surface area of water?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Container", yl: "Mass of water left (g)", bars: [["A", 70], ["B", 45], ["C", 85], ["D", 60]] }, o: ["A", "B", "C", "D"], a: 1, why: "Only the shape changed. B lost the most water (55 g), so it evaporated fastest, which means it had the largest exposed surface area.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Crushed ice at 0 °C was heated steadily. The graph shows its temperature. During which period was the ice melting?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 0], ["2", 0], ["4", 0], ["6", 14], ["8", 28], ["10", 42]] }, o: ["0 min to 4 min", "4 min to 10 min", "6 min to 10 min", "Only at 4 min"], a: 0, why: "The flat part at 0 °C shows melting. After 4 min all the ice has melted, so the water’s temperature rises.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Ahmad walked out of an air-conditioned MRT station into the hot afternoon. His spectacles misted up. Why?", o: ["Water vapour in the warm air lost heat to the cooler lenses and condensed on them.", "Sweat from his face evaporated, then froze into ice on the cold lenses.", "The cold lenses gave out water vapour, which then turned into tiny droplets on them.", "The lenses gained heat outside, so water inside the lenses came out onto them."], a: 0, why: "The lenses were cooled in the station. Water vapour in the warmer air outside lost heat to them and condensed into tiny droplets.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Two identical wet cloths were hung in the same room as shown. Cloth A dried first. Why?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "box", lines: ["Wet cloth", "Under a fan"] }, { label: "B", icon: "box", lines: ["Wet cloth", "No fan"] }] }, o: ["The moving air from the fan made the water on cloth A boil away.", "The moving air increased the rate of evaporation from cloth A.", "The fan made the air around cloth A more humid, so it dried faster.", "The fan cooled cloth A, so the water on it condensed and dripped off."], a: 1, why: "Wind or moving air increases the rate of evaporation. The water does not boil; it evaporates at room temperature.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Sweating helps to keep us cool on a hot day. Why?", o: ["Sweat condenses on the skin and gains heat.", "Sweat loses heat to the body when it evaporates.", "Sweat gains heat from the body when it evaporates.", "Sweat blocks heat from the Sun."], a: 2, why: "As sweat evaporates, it gains heat from the body, so the body loses heat and cools down.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "The same clothes take longer to dry outdoors on a rainy day than on a dry, sunny day. Which of these are reasons? A: The air is more humid. B: The temperature of the surroundings is lower. C: The exposed surface area of the clothes is smaller.", o: ["A only", "B and C only", "A, B and C", "A and B only"], a: 3, why: "On a rainy day the air holds more water vapour and is cooler, so evaporation is slower. The clothes are the same, so their exposed surface area has not changed.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Mei poured some syrup water into moulds and left them in the freezer overnight to make ice lollies. What happened to the syrup water?", o: ["It gained heat and froze.", "It lost heat and melted.", "It lost heat and froze.", "It gained heat and condensed."], a: 2, why: "The freezer is colder, so the syrup water loses heat and freezes into a solid.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Nurul wants to find out if the temperature of the surroundings affects the rate of evaporation of water. Which two set-ups should she compare?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "50 ml water", "In the sun"] }, { label: "B", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "50 ml water", "In the shade"] }, { label: "C", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Narrow beaker", "50 ml water", "In the sun"] }, { label: "D", icon: "dish", lines: ["Wide dish", "100 ml water", "In the shade"] }] }, o: ["A and B", "A and C", "B and D", "C and D"], a: 0, why: "A and B differ only in the place (sun vs shade), which changes the temperature. A and C test surface area, B and D test the amount of water, and C and D change more than one thing.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "After a hot shower, the bathroom mirror became misty. Which best explains this?", o: ["Hot water from the shower splashed onto the mirror and then froze on it.", "Water vapour in the air lost heat to the cooler mirror and condensed into tiny droplets.", "The mirror gained heat from the shower and gave out water from inside the glass.", "Water vapour gained heat from the mirror and turned into tiny water droplets."], a: 1, why: "Water vapour from the hot shower loses heat to the cooler mirror and condenses. Water vapour loses heat, not gains it, when it condenses.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Four identical wet towels were hung in different places. The graph shows the time each took to dry. Which conclusion is supported by the results?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Place", yl: "Time taken to dry (h)", bars: [["Sunny, windy", 2], ["Sunny, still air", 3], ["Shady, windy", 4], ["Shady, still air", 6]] }, o: ["Towels dry faster in shady places.", "Wind has no effect on drying time.", "Towels dry fastest in a sunny, windy place.", "Towels can dry only in sunny places."], a: 2, why: "The shortest time (2 h) was in the sunny, windy place. In both sun and shade, wind shortened the drying time, and the towels in the shade still dried.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Which of these involve water LOSING heat? A: Melting B: Freezing C: Evaporation D: Condensation", o: ["A and C only", "B and D only", "A and B only", "C and D only"], a: 1, why: "Freezing (liquid to solid) and condensation (gas to liquid) involve losing heat. Melting and evaporation involve gaining heat.", lvl: 2 },
    { q: "Some water was placed in a freezer. The graph shows its temperature. What was happening from 10 min to 25 min?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 20], ["5", 10], ["10", 0], ["15", 0], ["20", 0], ["25", 0]] }, o: ["The water was gaining heat and melting.", "The water was neither gaining nor losing heat.", "The water was boiling.", "The water was losing heat and freezing."], a: 3, why: "The flat part at 0 °C shows freezing. The water keeps losing heat to the colder freezer even though its temperature stays at 0 °C.", lvl: 2 },

    { q: "A pot of water is boiling on a stove. The graph shows its temperature. At 10 min the flame was turned up. What is the most likely temperature of the water at 14 min?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 30], ["2", 60], ["4", 90], ["6", 100], ["8", 100], ["10", 100]] }, o: ["Above 100 °C, and the water boils away faster", "100 °C, and the water boils away faster", "100 °C, and the water boils away more slowly", "Below 100 °C, because it is boiling away"], a: 1, why: "Once water boils, its temperature stays at 100 °C. A bigger flame gives more heat, which changes water into water vapour faster, but does not raise the temperature.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Water in a beaker was heated steadily. Study the graph. Which statement is NOT correct?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 25], ["3", 50], ["6", 75], ["9", 100], ["12", 100], ["15", 100]] }, o: ["The water started to boil at about 9 min, when it reached 100 °C.", "Between 9 min and 15 min the water was still gaining heat from the flame.", "Between 9 min and 15 min the water gained no heat, as its temperature did not rise.", "The boiling point of the water is 100 °C, where its temperature levelled off."], a: 2, why: "The flame was still heating the water, so it was still gaining heat. The heat was used to change water into water vapour, which is why the temperature stayed at 100 °C.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A glass of iced water was placed on an electronic balance in a classroom. Study the readings. Why did the mass increase?", tbl: [["Time (min)", "0", "10", "20"], ["Mass (g)", "350", "352", "354"]], o: ["Air from the room passed into the glass through its walls and added mass.", "Water vapour in the air condensed on the cooler outer surface of the glass.", "The ice melted into water, which is heavier than the same amount of ice.", "The iced water evaporated and the water vapour formed added to the mass."], a: 1, why: "Water droplets from the surrounding air condensed on the outside of the glass and added to its mass. Melting does not change mass, and evaporation would reduce it.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which of these will increase the rate at which water evaporates from a dish? A: Placing it in a warmer place B: Pouring the water into a wider dish C: Placing it in more humid air D: Placing it under a fan", o: ["A and B only", "B, C and D only", "A, B, C and D", "A, B and D only"], a: 3, why: "Higher temperature, larger exposed surface area and moving air increase the rate. More humid air DECREASES the rate of evaporation.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "When a kettle boils, there is a clear gap X just above the spout, then a white mist Y further away. What are X and Y?", o: ["X: water vapour; Y: tiny water droplets", "X: tiny water droplets; Y: water vapour", "X: air only; Y: water vapour", "X and Y: water vapour"], a: 0, why: "Hot, invisible water vapour leaves the spout (X). A little further away, it loses heat to the cooler air and condenses into visible tiny droplets (Y).", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Hui Min set up the experiment shown to find out if exposed surface area affects the rate of evaporation. The two cloths were the same size and soaked with the same amount of water. Why is her experiment NOT a fair test?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "plate", lines: ["Wet cloth spread out", "In the sun"] }, { label: "B", icon: "plate", lines: ["Wet cloth folded", "In the shade"] }] }, o: ["The cloths are different sizes.", "More than one variable was changed.", "Cloths cannot be used to study evaporation.", "The cloths should hold different amounts of water."], a: 1, why: "Both the exposed surface area (spread vs folded) and the place (sun vs shade) were changed, so she cannot tell which one caused the difference.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Which of these are examples of condensation? A: Dew on grass in the early morning B: Wet hair drying C: Droplets on the underside of a pot lid D: Water in an ice tray turning solid", o: ["A and C only", "B and D only", "A, B and C only", "C only"], a: 0, why: "Dew and droplets under a lid form when water vapour loses heat to a cooler surface. Hair drying is evaporation; ice forming is freezing.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "A wet cloth was placed in front of a fan set at different speeds. The graph shows the mass of water lost from the cloth in 1 hour. Which conclusion is correct?", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Fan speed", yl: "Mass of water lost in 1 h (g)", bars: [["Off", 4], ["Low", 9], ["Medium", 14], ["High", 19]] }, o: ["As the speed of the moving air increases, the rate of evaporation increases.", "As the speed of the moving air increases, the rate of evaporation decreases.", "The variable changed was the temperature of the air.", "Fan speed has no effect on the rate of evaporation."], a: 0, why: "More water was lost in the same time as the fan speed increased, so faster moving air gives a faster rate of evaporation.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "On a hot, rainy afternoon, Mr Ong turned on the air-conditioner in his car and the windscreen became very cold. Water droplets formed on the windscreen. On which surface did they form, and why?", o: ["Inner surface, because water vapour in the air inside the cool car condenses on it", "Both surfaces, because water from the rain passes through the glass to the inside", "Outer surface, because water vapour in the warm, humid air outside condenses on the cool glass", "Outer surface, because the cold glass makes the raindrops on it freeze and stick"], a: 2, why: "The warm, humid air outside is the source of water vapour, and the windscreen is cooler than it. The air-conditioned air inside is cool and dry.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Mr Lim’s spectacles misted up (i) when he bent over a hot bowl of bak kut teh and (ii) when he stepped out of an air-conditioned room into the warm corridor. Which statement is correct for BOTH cases?", o: ["Water vapour lost heat to the lenses, which were cooler than it, and condensed.", "The lenses were warmer than the water vapour.", "In (i) the water vapour came from the corridor air; in (ii) it came from the soup.", "Water evaporated from the lenses and stayed on them."], a: 0, why: "In both cases water vapour (from the soup in (i), from the corridor air in (ii)) came into contact with cooler lenses, lost heat and condensed.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Crushed ice at 0 °C was heated steadily. The graph shows its temperature. Which correctly describes what was in the beaker at 2 min and at 8 min?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 0], ["2", 0], ["4", 0], ["6", 18], ["8", 36], ["10", 54]] }, o: ["2 min: ice only; 8 min: ice and water", "2 min: water only; 8 min: ice and water", "2 min: ice and water; 8 min: boiling water", "2 min: ice and water; 8 min: liquid water only"], a: 3, why: "At 2 min the temperature is flat at 0 °C, so ice is melting and both ice and water are present. At 8 min the temperature is rising (36 °C), so all the ice has melted but the water is far below its boiling point.", lvl: 3 },
    { q: "Two dishes, each with 50 ml of water, were placed in the same room as shown. What would you expect after 2 days, and why?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "box", lines: ["Dish of 50 ml water", "In a covered tank", "Air very humid"] }, { label: "B", icon: "box", lines: ["Dish of 50 ml water", "In an open tank", "Air dry"] }] }, o: ["A has less water left, because humid air makes water evaporate faster.", "Both have the same amount left, because they are at the same temperature.", "B has less water left, because water evaporates faster into less humid air.", "B has more water left, because dry air stops evaporation."], a: 2, why: "The drier the surrounding air, the faster the rate of evaporation. Humid air already holds a lot of water vapour, so evaporation from A is slower.", lvl: 3 },

    { q: "A beaker of ice and water was stirred and left in a room at 30 °C. The graph shows its temperature. Which best explains the graph from 0 min to 6 min?", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 0], ["2", 0], ["4", 0], ["6", 0], ["8", 4], ["10", 8]] }, o: ["The beaker did not gain any heat from the room, so the temperature stayed at 0 °C.", "Heat gained from the room was used to melt the ice, so the temperature stayed at 0 °C.", "The ice lost heat to the water, so the temperature of the mixture did not change.", "The water was freezing into ice, so the temperature stayed at 0 °C the whole time."], a: 1, why: "The room is warmer, so the mixture gains heat all the time. While ice is still present, the heat is used to melt it, so the temperature stays at 0 °C. Only after all the ice has melted (about 6 min) does the temperature rise.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Three thermometers were placed in the same room: A was dry, B had its bulb wrapped in a wet cloth, and C had its bulb wrapped in a wet cloth and was placed in front of a fan blowing air at room temperature. The graph shows their readings after 15 min. Which statements are correct? P: Water in the cloths gained heat from the bulbs as it evaporated. Q: The fan increased the rate of evaporation, so more heat was removed from bulb C. R: C was lowest because the fan blew cold air. S: B was lower than A because water vapour condensed on the cloth.", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Thermometer", yl: "Reading (°C)", bars: [["A (dry)", 30], ["B (wet cloth)", 27], ["C (wet cloth + fan)", 24]] }, o: ["P only", "P and Q only", "Q and R only", "P, Q and S only"], a: 1, why: "Evaporation removes heat from the bulb (P), and faster evaporation removes heat faster (Q). The fan’s air was at room temperature, so R is wrong; condensation would give heat to the bulb, so S is wrong.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Two identical uncovered glasses were placed on balances in the same classroom. Glass X held iced water and glass Y held water at room temperature. Study the set-ups. Which statement is correct?", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "X", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Iced water", "Start: 300 g", "After 30 min: 306 g"] }, { label: "Y", icon: "beaker", lines: ["Room-temp water", "Start: 300 g", "After 30 min: 299 g"] }] }, o: ["Water droplets formed only on the outside of X; Y lost water by evaporation.", "Both glasses gained mass because water vapour condensed on their outer surfaces.", "X gained mass because the ice turned into water; Y’s mass stayed the same.", "Y lost mass because its water boiled; X’s mass stayed the same throughout."], a: 0, why: "Only X was cooler than the surrounding air, so water vapour condensed on it and added mass. Y was not cooler, so no droplets formed; it lost mass as water evaporated. Melting does not change mass and Y did not reach 100 °C.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Beakers P and Q were heated over identical flames. Study the table. At 20 min, the flame under Q was made bigger. Which is correct?", tbl: [["Beaker", "Volume of water", "Time to reach 100 °C", "Temperature at 20 min"], ["P", "200 ml", "6 min", "100 °C"], ["Q", "400 ml", "12 min", "100 °C"]], o: ["Q’s temperature rises above 100 °C because it is gaining more heat.", "Q boils at a higher temperature because it has more water in it.", "Q’s temperature stays at 100 °C but its water turns into water vapour faster.", "Q’s temperature drops because more of its water boils away faster."], a: 2, why: "The volume only changes how long the water takes to reach 100 °C. Water boils at 100 °C regardless of volume, and extra heat during boiling changes water into water vapour faster without raising the temperature.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "A wet cloth on a dish was sealed inside a clear plastic bag and placed on a balance. After 1 hour, the set-up was moved into a cooler air-conditioned room and water droplets appeared on the inner surface of the bag. Which statement is correct?", o: ["The mass decreased because water evaporated from the cloth.", "The mass stayed the same because the water vapour could not escape from the bag.", "The droplets came from water vapour in the air-conditioned room.", "No evaporation took place inside the sealed bag."], a: 1, why: "Water evaporated from the cloth, but the water vapour stayed inside the sealed bag and later condensed on the cooler inner surface, so the total mass did not change. Air outside the bag cannot reach the inner surface.", lvl: 4 },
    { q: "Two identical wet cloths were hung at the same temperature: one in a small closed room, one in an open corridor. Study the table. Which best explains why the cloth in the closed room lost less water each hour?", tbl: [["Hour", "1st", "2nd", "3rd", "4th"], ["Water lost in closed room (g)", "30", "22", "15", "9"], ["Water lost in open corridor (g)", "30", "30", "29", "28"]], o: ["The air in the closed room became more humid as water vapour from the cloth collected in it.", "The cloth in the closed room had less water left in it than the cloth in the corridor.", "The temperature in the closed room kept rising.", "Water vapour in the closed room froze on the cloth."], a: 0, why: "The corridor cloth lost MORE water, so it had even less left, yet its rate hardly dropped — so ‘less water left’ is not the reason. In the closed room, water vapour could not escape, the air became more humid and evaporation slowed.", lvl: 4 }
  ],
  tf: [
    { s: "Water vapour is invisible.", a: true, why: "Water vapour is a gas that cannot be seen." },
    { s: "The white ‘steam’ seen above a boiling kettle is water vapour.", a: false, why: "It is tiny water droplets formed by condensation; water vapour is invisible." },
    { s: "Evaporation can take place at room temperature.", a: true, why: "Evaporation takes place at any temperature." },
    { s: "Evaporation takes place throughout the whole body of water.", a: false, why: "Evaporation takes place only at the exposed surface; boiling takes place throughout the liquid." },
    { s: "As long as boiling water is heated, its temperature keeps rising.", a: false, why: "Its temperature stays at 100 °C; the heat is used to change water into water vapour." },
    { s: "Ice melts at 0 °C and water freezes at 0 °C.", a: true, why: "The melting point and the freezing point of water are the same, 0 °C." },
    { s: "Water vapour must gain heat to condense.", a: false, why: "Water vapour loses heat to a cooler surface when it condenses." },
    { s: "Water droplets on the outside of a glass of iced water come from the water vapour in the surrounding air.", a: true, why: "The water vapour loses heat to the cooler outer surface and condenses." },
    { s: "Water droplets form on the outside of a cold can because the drink seeps through the can.", a: false, why: "Water cannot pass through the can; the droplets come from water vapour in the air." },
    { s: "Clothes dry more slowly on a humid day.", a: true, why: "Humid air already contains a lot of water vapour, so the rate of evaporation is lower." },
    { s: "Folding a wet towel makes it dry faster.", a: false, why: "Folding reduces the exposed surface area, so the water evaporates more slowly." },
    { s: "Sweat cools the body because it gains heat from the body as it evaporates.", a: true, why: "The body loses the heat that the sweat gains, so it cools down." },
    { s: "A fan makes us feel cooler because it blows out cold air.", a: false, why: "The fan’s air is at room temperature; it increases the rate of evaporation of sweat." },
    { s: "While ice is melting, it gains heat but its temperature stays at 0 °C.", a: true, why: "The heat gained is used to change the ice into water." },
    { s: "Water can evaporate only when it is heated by the Sun.", a: false, why: "Water evaporates at any temperature, even indoors or at night." },
    { s: "The water droplets on the underside of the lid of a pot of hot soup are formed by condensation.", a: true, why: "Water vapour from the soup loses heat to the cooler lid and condenses." },
    { s: "When water freezes, it gains heat.", a: false, why: "Water loses heat when it freezes." },
    { s: "Boiling takes place at a fixed temperature, but evaporation can take place at any temperature.", a: true, why: "Water boils at 100 °C but evaporates at any temperature." },
    { s: "A puddle in a deep, narrow hole dries faster than the same amount of water spread thinly over the floor.", a: false, why: "The thin layer has a larger exposed surface area, so it evaporates faster." },
    { s: "The mass of a glass of iced water left uncovered in a warm room can increase over time.", a: true, why: "Water vapour from the air condenses on the outer surface and adds to the mass." }
  ],
  sort: [
    { title: "In which state is the water?", groups: ["Solid", "Liquid", "Gas"], items: [["ice cube in a drink", 0], ["hailstone", 0], ["shaved ice in ice kacang", 0], ["rain drop", 1], ["sweat on the skin", 1], ["dew on a leaf", 1], ["white mist above a boiling kettle", 1], ["water vapour in the air", 2], ["clear gap just above a kettle spout", 2], ["bubbles inside boiling water", 2]] },
    { title: "Does the water gain heat or lose heat?", groups: ["Gains heat", "Loses heat"], items: [["ice cube melting in a drink", 0], ["wet clothes drying", 0], ["water boiling in a kettle", 0], ["sweat evaporating", 0], ["puddle drying up", 0], ["water in an ice tray freezing", 1], ["droplets forming on a cold can", 1], ["bathroom mirror misting", 1], ["dew forming on grass at night", 1], ["spectacles misting over hot soup", 1]] },
    { title: "Does the change make water evaporate faster or slower?", groups: ["Faster", "Slower"], items: [["spreading out wet clothes", 0], ["moving the drying rack under a fan", 0], ["hanging clothes in a sunny spot", 0], ["pouring the water into a wider dish", 0], ["opening windows on a windy day", 0], ["hanging clothes out on a humid, rainy day", 1], ["folding a wet towel in half", 1], ["covering a dish of water with a lid", 1], ["moving the dish into a cool, shady room", 1], ["pouring the water into a narrow bottle", 1]] }
  ],
  oe: [
    { q: "A pot of water was heated over a steady flame. The graph shows its temperature. (a) At what temperature did the water boil? (b) The flame was still lit from 6 min to 10 min. Explain why the temperature of the water did not change during this time.", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 40], ["2", 60], ["4", 80], ["6", 100], ["8", 100], ["10", 100]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["100"], ["gain", "absorb"], ["water vapour", "gas"]], model: "(a) 100 °C. (b) The water was still gaining heat from the flame, but the heat gained was used to change the liquid water into water vapour, so its temperature stayed the same." },
    { q: "When a kettle boils, there is a clear gap just above the spout and a white mist a short distance away. (a) What is found in the clear gap? (b) Explain how the white mist is formed.", marks: 3, kw: [["water vapour"], ["lose heat", "loses heat", "lost heat", "gives out heat", "cooler"], ["droplet"]], model: "(a) Invisible water vapour. (b) The hot water vapour loses heat to the cooler surrounding air and condenses into tiny water droplets, which we see as the white mist." },
    { q: "Siti placed a glass of iced water on an electronic balance. Study the table. (a) Where did the water droplets on the outer surface of the glass come from? (b) Explain how the droplets were formed. (c) Explain why the reading on the balance increased.", tbl: [["Time (min)", "0", "10", "20"], ["Mass (g)", "350", "352", "354"], ["Outer surface", "Dry", "A few droplets", "Many droplets"]], marks: 4, kw: [["surrounding air", "the air", "air around"], ["lose heat", "loses heat", "lost heat", "lost its heat"], ["condense"], ["add", "extra", "mass of the droplets", "weight of the droplets"]], model: "(a) From the water vapour in the surrounding air. (b) The water vapour came into contact with the cooler outer surface of the glass, lost heat and condensed into water droplets. (c) The water droplets that formed on the outer surface added to the mass of the glass." },
    { q: "Hana soaked two identical towels with the same amount of water and hung them side by side in the same corridor as shown. (a) What is Hana trying to find out? (b) Which towel will dry first? Explain why.", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "A", icon: "box", lines: ["Wet towel", "Spread out fully"] }, { label: "B", icon: "box", lines: ["Wet towel", "Folded into four"] }] }, marks: 3, kw: [["surface area"], ["towel a", "a will", "a dries", "a would", "spread"], ["faster", "higher rate", "greater rate", "quicker", "more quickly"]], model: "(a) She is finding out whether the exposed surface area of the towel affects the rate of evaporation. (b) Towel A will dry first. It is spread out fully, so it has a larger exposed surface area and the water evaporates faster." },
    { q: "After a game of football, Jun Wei sat under a fan and felt cooler. (a) What change of state took place on his skin? (b) Explain why he felt cooler. (c) Explain how the fan helped.", marks: 3, kw: [["evaporat"], ["gain", "absorb", "took heat", "takes heat", "removes heat", "removed heat"], ["moving air", "wind", "faster", "rate"]], model: "(a) The sweat on his skin evaporated. (b) As the sweat evaporated, it gained heat from his body, so his body lost heat and he felt cooler. (c) The moving air from the fan increased the rate of evaporation of the sweat." },
    { q: "Mr Tan walked out of an air-conditioned shopping mall into the hot afternoon. His spectacles misted up. Explain why.", marks: 2, kw: [["cooler", "colder"], ["condense"]], model: "The lenses were cooler than the warm surrounding air. Water vapour in the surrounding air lost heat to the cooler lenses and condensed into tiny water droplets on them." },
    { q: "Explain why clothes hung outdoors in Singapore take longer to dry on a rainy day.", marks: 2, kw: [["humid", "more water vapour"], ["slow", "lower rate", "less water evaporates"]], model: "On a rainy day the air is more humid, as it already contains more water vapour. So the rate of evaporation of water from the clothes is slower and the clothes take longer to dry." },
    { q: "Four identical wet towels were hung in different places. The graph shows the time each took to dry. (a) Which place is best for drying clothes? (b) Using the results, state two factors that affect how fast the towels dried. (c) Explain why the towel in the shady place with still air took the longest.", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Place", yl: "Time taken to dry (h)", bars: [["Sunny, windy", 2], ["Sunny, still air", 3], ["Shady, windy", 4], ["Shady, still air", 6]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["sunny, windy", "sunny and windy", "sunny windy"], ["temperature", "sunlight", "amount of sun", "sun or shade", "heat"], ["moving air", "air movement", "presence of wind", "amount of wind", "wind speed", "whether there is wind"], ["slow", "lower rate", "least"]], model: "(a) The sunny, windy place. (b) The temperature of the surroundings (sun or shade) and wind (moving air). (c) The shady place had a lower temperature and there was no moving air, so the rate of evaporation was the slowest." },
    { q: "Some water was placed in a freezer. The graph shows its temperature. (a) At what temperature did the water freeze? (b) Explain why the temperature stayed the same from 10 min to 25 min. (c) If the water were left in the freezer for another hour, predict what would happen to its temperature once all of it had frozen.", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 20], ["5", 10], ["10", 0], ["15", 0], ["20", 0], ["25", 0]] }, marks: 3, kw: [["0"], ["los", "lost"], ["drop", "decrease", "fall", "lower", "below"]], model: "(a) 0 °C. (b) The water was still losing heat to the colder freezer, but it was changing from liquid water into ice, so its temperature stayed at 0 °C. (c) The temperature would drop below 0 °C once all the water had frozen, as the ice continues to lose heat." },
    { q: "State two differences between evaporation and boiling.", marks: 2, kw: [["any temperature"], ["surface", "throughout"]], model: "Evaporation can take place at any temperature, but boiling takes place only at the boiling point of 100 °C. Evaporation takes place only at the exposed surface of the water, but boiling takes place throughout the water." },
    { q: "Three thermometers were placed in the same room. A was dry. B had its bulb wrapped in a wet cloth. C had its bulb wrapped in a wet cloth and was placed in front of a fan blowing air at room temperature. The graph shows the readings after 15 minutes. (a) Explain why B showed a lower reading than A. (b) Explain why C showed the lowest reading. (c) Predict the reading of C after the cloth has become completely dry. Give a reason.", fig: { type: "bar", xl: "Thermometer", yl: "Reading (°C)", bars: [["A (dry)", 30], ["B (wet cloth)", 27], ["C (wet cloth + fan)", 24]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["gain", "absorb", "took heat", "takes heat", "removed heat", "removes heat"], ["rate", "faster"], ["30"], ["no more water", "no water", "no evaporation", "stops evaporating", "nothing to evaporate", "no more evaporation"]], model: "(a) Water in the wet cloth evaporated and gained heat from the bulb of B, so the bulb lost heat. (b) The moving air from the fan increased the rate of evaporation, so heat was removed from the bulb of C faster. (c) About 30 °C, the same as A, because there is no more water to evaporate, so no heat is removed from the bulb and it gains heat from the room.", x: 1 },
    { q: "A wet cloth on a dish was sealed in a clear plastic bag and placed on a balance, as shown. After 1 hour it was moved into a cooler air-conditioned room, and water droplets appeared on the inner surface of the bag. (a) Explain how the droplets on the inner surface were formed. (b) Predict how the balance reading after 2 hours compares with the reading at the start. Explain your answer.", fig: { type: "setups", items: [{ label: "Start", icon: "plate", lines: ["Wet cloth on a dish", "Sealed in plastic bag", "On a balance", "In a warm room"] }, { label: "After 1 h", icon: "plate", lines: ["Same set-up", "Moved to a cool room", "Droplets inside bag"] }] }, marks: 4, kw: [["evaporat"], ["lose heat", "loses heat", "lost heat", "cooler"], ["same", "no change", "unchanged"], ["escape", "sealed", "trapped", "stayed inside"]], model: "(a) Water in the cloth evaporated into water vapour inside the bag. When the bag was moved to the cooler room, the water vapour lost heat to the cooler inner surface of the bag and condensed into water droplets. (b) The reading stays the same, because the bag is sealed and the water vapour cannot escape, so no water is lost from the set-up.", x: 1 },
    { q: "A beaker of ice and water was stirred and left in a room at 30 °C. The graph shows its temperature. (a) Explain why the temperature stayed at 0 °C from 0 min to 6 min. (b) Why did the temperature start to rise after 6 min? (c) Predict the temperature of the water after a very long time.", fig: { type: "line", xl: "Time (min)", yl: "Temperature (°C)", pts: [["0", 0], ["2", 0], ["4", 0], ["6", 0], ["8", 4], ["10", 8]] }, marks: 4, kw: [["gain", "absorb"], ["melt"], ["all the ice", "no more ice", "no ice", "ice had melted", "ice has melted", "ice was melted"], ["30"]], model: "(a) The mixture was gaining heat from the warmer room, but the heat gained was used to melt the ice, so the temperature stayed at 0 °C. (b) By 6 min all the ice had melted, so the heat gained raised the temperature of the water. (c) About 30 °C, the same as the room temperature.", x: 1 },
    { q: "Dishes washed at a hawker centre are placed upright on a rack in a well-ventilated area instead of being stacked on top of one another. Explain how this helps them to dry faster.", marks: 2, kw: [["surface area", "exposed"], ["moving air", "wind", "air can flow", "air flows"]], model: "Placing the dishes apart on the rack gives the water on them a larger exposed surface area, and the moving air in the well-ventilated area increases the rate of evaporation." }
  ],
  doc: [
    { q: "Water droplets formed on the outer surface of a glass of iced water. Explain how they were formed.", marks: 2, answers: ["The water inside the glass seeped out through the glass and became droplets.", "Water vapour in the surrounding air lost heat to the cooler outer surface of the glass and condensed into water droplets.", "Condensation happened because the glass is cold."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 names the source (water vapour in the surrounding air), the heat loss to the cooler surface and condensation. Answer 1 is a misconception — water cannot pass through glass. Answer 3 only names the process; it gives no source and no heat loss." },
    { q: "Explain why the temperature of boiling water stays at 100 °C although it is still being heated.", marks: 2, answers: ["No more heat goes into the water once it boils.", "The water has reached its highest temperature.", "The water is still gaining heat, but the heat is used to change the water into water vapour."], best: 2, why: "Answer 3 says heat is still gained AND what the heat is used for. Answer 1 is wrong — the water keeps gaining heat. Answer 2 just restates the observation without a reason." },
    { q: "Why do we feel cooler when we sit under a fan after exercising?", marks: 2, answers: ["The moving air increases the rate of evaporation of sweat, and the sweat gains heat from our body as it evaporates.", "The fan blows cold air onto our body.", "The sweat evaporates."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 links the fan to a faster rate of evaporation AND says the sweat gains heat from the body. Answer 2 is a misconception — the air is not colder. Answer 3 names evaporation but not the heat gained from the body or the role of the fan." },
    { q: "The white ‘steam’ above a pot of boiling soup is not water vapour. What is it and how is it formed?", marks: 2, answers: ["It is tiny water droplets. Water vapour from the soup loses heat to the cooler air and condenses.", "It is smoke from the hot stove.", "It is water vapour that has become visible because it is hot."], best: 0, why: "Answer 1 identifies tiny water droplets and explains condensation with heat loss. Answer 2 confuses it with smoke. Answer 3 contradicts the fact that water vapour is always invisible." },
    { q: "Wei Ling found that a wet cloth spread out dried faster than an identical wet cloth that was folded. Explain why.", marks: 2, answers: ["The spread-out cloth is bigger, so it dries faster.", "The spread-out cloth has a larger exposed surface area, so the water evaporates faster.", "The folded cloth has more water."], best: 1, why: "Answer 2 uses the key phrase ‘larger exposed surface area’ and links it to a faster rate of evaporation. Answer 1 is wrong — the cloths are the same size. Answer 3 is wrong — both hold the same amount of water." }
  ]
};
